- op: discuss  # ea5c
  at: '2026-11-22T23:47:02Z'  # ea5c
  by: agent  # ea5c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ea5c
  clock: 4  # ea5c
  nonce: ea5c  # ea5c
  message: "SECOND TRANCHE AND THE DETECTORS, PR #2342. Every one of the 164 deleted rows now has a measured verdict, 158 of the open forms have a detector, and the rest are filed by mechanism.\n\nTRIAGE (docs/audits/0029; method as audit 0028). The 136 rows that were not `route` rows are 348 forms, verdicts on the tree BEFORE the PR: DETECTED 13, PARTLY 66, NOT DETECTED 185, NO USER CODE 83 (a library constructor, a configuration call, a helper: nothing of the repository is registered), not measured 1 (go:W7). Six groups, one fixture per framework, a real repository where one exists locally.\n\nBUILT (35 commits). One mechanism for most of it: an analyzer that finds a call handing LOCAL code to a library asks framework_patterns.usage_row_names_call(name, language, position) whether a usage row is written against that call at that argument, and records a usage context only then; the row stamps the concept. No table of framework call names in an analyzer: a new row is enough. JavaScript / TypeScript, Python, Go, Ruby and Elixir record such contexts. Elixir modules record what they `use` (meta adopted_modules), read by a new row key `adopts:` (also Ruby's included_modules). Scala / Kotlin: program-start rows, a Ktor module row, two Scala call-graph fixes. Core: find_non_test_files classified a file by its ABSOLUTE path, so a checkout under a folder named test/ or fixtures/ lost every content-scanning linker's output (found because the triage fixtures sat under fixtures/).\n158 of the 251 forms that were NOT DETECTED or PARTLY are served (the audit lists them by id and mechanism; counted from the work packages' lists, each id checked against the tables as open).\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE (old code c8b4180d0 against the branch, one run per arm, 21 repositories; movur_dibin_11222026/movur/ab/): declared symbols gone 0 and stable ids moved 0 on every tree; no change at all on 9 trees in untouched languages. beads: 56 cobra handlers carry `command`, 52 are cli_command entries (0 before). plausible: 31 background_task, 32 LiveView, 24 middleware_handler, 5 error_handler entries (0 before). polis server: 7 middleware_handler. knex: 11 cli_command. tapir: 66 `@main` program starts. Work packages' own runs: mlflow 67 of 67 click subcommands (1 before); gitlab app/graphql 610 resolvers; mastodon 16 Thor subcommand classes and 91 Sidekiq workers; workadventure electron 13 IPC handlers. Tests run the real analyzers on source and the shipped rows through enrich_symbols; 146 mutation controls (18 core, 128 work packages): 145 red, 1 equivalent mutant.\n\nDECISIONS TAKEN THAT ARE THE OWNER'S TO OVERTURN.\n1. Rails callback rows (before_action ...) deleted, not fed: 527 new entries on one controller tree, and the targets already have a caller.\n2. The Phoenix controller-module row was not restored: it pushed 33 of 49 routes out of plausible's capped list and no controller there is unrouted.\n3. Even so, on Phoenix applications fewer routes are LISTED (plausible 49 -> 28, livebook 34 -> 26): every kept entry ties at rank 1.0 and ties are dealt one per source file, while routes share router.ex. The rank formula was not touched.\n4. A fastify websocket route stays http_route: recording method WS moves the route marker's stable id.\n5. Inline handlers (blocks, closures, lambdas without a symbol) get no symbol and no label.\n6. Rows dropped for a label that did not fit: argparse Action.__call__, aiohttp_jinja2 context processors; CLI hook fields (PersistentPreRun ...) got no row.\n7. The middleware-chain linker now chains the newly detected middleware of a file in DEFINITION order (5 edges on polis that are not the registration order). Not changed.\n\nRESIDUALS FILED: WI-nojin (the entry cap deals ties per file: an owner question), WI-bihuf (rows withheld for the entry list: Rails callbacks, Phoenix controller modules; owner), WI-tikiv (a class entry does not reach its methods in dead-code-maybe; owner), WI-rikum (JavaScript), WI-vurat (Go), WI-bupam (Ruby), WI-talan (Elixir), WI-kigaf (library_export on Elixir applications), WI-sipah (Scala / Kotlin), WI-vafas (Python, PHP, Swift, Rust), WI-fisof (middleware-chain linker order), WI-fadot (a near-full local test run loses a worker to a memory burst). WI-pahom (Electron IPC handlers) is closed by the same PR with its own repro.\nNotes posted on WI-tavum (inline handlers), WI-dopuh (what the route item gains and still lacks), WI-babuv (the Go reachability mechanism, measured).\n\nNOT COVERED. No local repository for hapi, @koa/router, vorpal, gluegun, kong, Sinatra, GLI, Commander, dry-cli, Ktor, akka-http applications, Slim, ariadne, bottle plugins: those rows rest on fixtures. dead-code-maybe and test-coverage output were not compared between the arms. After the 21-tree run two source commits joined the branch (an evidence type on Scala value references, a metadata key removed from JavaScript contexts); the run was not repeated.\n\nCLOSING AS DONE on the owner's rule \"close on the filed row, file the residual\": the item asked, per construct, for \"detected elsewhere / build a detector / not wanted\"; every form has that answer in audits 0028 and 0029, and each construct still without a detector is in one of the items above or in WI-dopuh / WI-mopuj. The general claim \"every framework construct that registers user code is detected\" is NOT asserted."  # ea5c
